.TH SH-UQ 1
.SH NAME
uq \- shell script interface to uq(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load uq

.B ${uq
.I spec template
.B }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Uq
is a loadable module for
.IR sh (1)
giving shell scripts direct access to
.IR uq (2)'s
Monte Carlo uncertainty-quantification sweep over a
.IR pde (2)
problem \(em a study naturally driven and reported from a shell
script, the same as
.IR sh-verify (1)'s
convergence check.
.PP
.I Spec
and
.I template
are each a single shell word, built the same way any other multi-line
spec string is assembled in
.IR sh (1)
scripts: quoted single-line fragments joined with
.BR sh (1)'s
own
.B ^
concatenation operator and a variable
.RI ( nl ,
say) holding one newline character.  Given such an
.IR nl :
.EX
	load uq
	template = 'mesh 20x20 domain 1x1 bc clamp'^$nl^'equation diffuse diffusivity $D'^$nl^'solver gmres tolerance 1e-8 restart 30'^$nl^'time step 0.01'
	spec = 'sample D uniform 0.03 0.08'^$nl^'count 20'^$nl^'init splat 10 10 0 1.0'^$nl^'until 0.5'^$nl^'observe get 10 10'
	result = ${uq $spec $template}
	echo $result
.EE
.I Spec
and
.I template
follow exactly
.IR uq (2)'s
own grammar, documented there in full.
.PP
.B ${uq}
yields one element carrying the summary \(em six space-separated
numbers,
.RI \(lq n " " nfailed " " mean " " stddev " " min " " max \(rq
\(em followed by
.I n
more elements, the raw quantity-of-interest value from each
successful draw, the same fields
.IR uq (2)'s
own
.B Summary
carries.  A malformed spec or template, or a template where every draw
fails, raises a
.B uq
exception with the same text
.IR uq (2)'s
.B run
would have returned.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/uq.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR uq (2),
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-pde (1),
.IR sh-verify (1)
